    <DataNode elementId="MONOMER-19024" textLabel="lysophospholipid acyltransferase 1" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="F4IDU4" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="600" centerY="875" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup, SRI, caspi">Acyl-CoA:lysophospholipid acyltransferase (LPLAT) enzymes have central roles in acyl editing of phospholipids. These enzymes catalyze acyl exchange between acyl-CoA and position &lt;i&gt;sn&lt;/i&gt;-2 of several phospholipids. The |FRAME: G-43765 LPLAT1| gene of |FRAME: TAX-3702 &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt;| encodes an enzyme that interacts with several phopholipids, including |FRAME: PHOSPHATIDYLCHOLINE &quot;&lt;i&gt;sn&lt;/i&gt;-phosphatidylcholin&quot;| (PC), |FRAME: L-1-PHOSPHATIDYL-ETHANOLAMINE phosphatidylethanolamine| (PE), and |FRAME: L-1-PHOSPHATIDYL-SERINE phosphatidylserine| (PS) |CITS: [18154737]|. It can also interact with |FRAME: L-PHOSPHATIDATE phosphatidylglycerol| (PG) &lt;i&gt;in vitro&lt;/i&gt; |CITS: [22233193]|. In the forward reaction the enzyme incorporates acyl-CoAs into lysophospholipids, where they are often subject to further desaturation. In the reverse direction the enzyme releases the polyunsaturated acyl-CoAs, enabling their incorporation into |FRAME: DIACYLGLYCEROL &quot;diacylglycerol&quot;| and |FRAME: Triacylglycerols triacylglycerol| via the |FRAME: TRIGLSYN-PWY &quot;Kennedy pathway&quot;|. The forward reaction involves the breaking of a thioester bond and forming an oxygen ester, and is strongly exothermic. Thus equilibrium of the LPCAT reaction favors the forward direction, and the reverse reaction requires that the pool sizes of acyl-CoAs is very small. The |FRAME: G-43765 LPLAT1| gene of |FRAME: TAX-3702 &lt;i&gt;Arabidopsis thaliana&lt;/i&gt;| was cloned and expressed in yeast and characterized biochemically in microsomal preparations of the cells |CITS: [24189065]|. In the forward direction the enzyme showed a preference for C18-unsaturated acyl-CoAs, and was able to acylate both &lt;i&gt;sn&lt;/i&gt; positions of PC, with a preference for the &lt;i&gt;sn&lt;/i&gt;-2 position. When acting in the reverse direction, the enzyme had similar activity with &lt;i&gt;sn&lt;/i&gt;-2-bound oleoyl, linoleoyl, and linolenoyl groups. It catalyzed the removal of a ricinoleoyl group 46-fold faster |CITS: [24189065]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="MONOMER-19024" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Araport" value="AT1G12640.1" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Uniprot" value="F4IDU4" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Interpro" value="IPR004299" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Pfam" value="PF03062" />
